WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.200
What I realized was being in an all-black school was a real benefit, I think, for me, I will say that,

2
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.440
because I think the support we had in the school,

3
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:23.880
a lot from the teachers as well as you’re in a community where you all know each other.

4
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.200
We’re from the community, and it was a small community, small school.

5
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.080
That also, I think, helped.

6
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.620
But the teachers were really good teachers.

7
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:40.320
They were really intent on trying to make us understand and teach us how to advance and go out into the world.

8
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.100
And we had a lot of that one on one.

9
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.760
There was a sense of pride.

10
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.740
Our teachers were very proud of who they were, their accomplishments, and that they were educators.

11
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.500
They dressed appropriately.

12
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.400
They demanded respect.

13
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.440
We were ordered and made sure they got respect.

14
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.500
Everybody from the bus driver to the janitors.

15
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:14.500
So it set up a respect and understanding of ourselves, and the fact that we happened to be African Americans or the minority,

16
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.560
that gave us a sense of pride in who we were.

17
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.920
When you’re accomplishing in an environment like that, then you get that sense of accomplishing.

18
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:36.180
After I started seeing what happened to minority children once the schools were completely integrated, they get lost in such a sea of everybody else.

19
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.180
And I think maybe in a lot of cases,

20
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:47.100
a genuine lack of understanding of what’s needed for these kids so that you can motivate them the right way and get them and turn them in the right direction,

21
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.740
that’s not there.

22
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.060
It gets missed.

23
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.980
When that happens, now you have a kid looking at, “Why am I here, I don't know why I’m here.

24
00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:01.420
I hate being . . .” If I was in a fog when I was in high school, surrounded by my community,

25
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:07.740
you can imagine what it might be like for a kid who’s maybe like me in an integrated community,

26
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.580
where nobody is stopping to say, “Hey, you’re okay here.

27
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.540
You’re safe.

28
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.600
You can feel comfortable here.”

29
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.440
And I think sometimes that’s what the problem is.

30
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:24.620
They don’t feel safe, they don’t feel comfortable, because it’s just too big, it’s too overwhelming, and it’s just not nurturing enough.

31
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.580
Maybe that just happened across the board overall.

32
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.880
But from a minority perspective, and what I got out of being at Mossville,

33
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.520
that’s what I see for the minority children who are now not in an environment like that.

34
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:47.180
So Mossville provided us not only an environment for education and for learning, but for confidence-building and for security, being in an all-black school.
